{
	title: 'Web tab switch is instant and shows the bottom',
	spec: 'Showing a tab on the web takes about one frame however long its history. The transcript renders only the newest rows first (40), and adds 60 earlier ones as the reader nears the top, per session, kept when the tab is shown again; the linked block (target) is always among the rows. Rows loaded earlier come in above without moving the view (scroll.anchor). Cards created by a tab switch or by earlier rows arriving do not fade in (scroll.quiet); only news fades. A tab whose reader was at the bottom shows its last messages, never scrollback. A catch-up (more than 8 new items in one change, as after a reconnect) snaps a bottom reader to the bottom within the same task: the rows are replaced and scrollTop resets, so a glide would sweep the whole session past the reader and the near-top load would strand the view mid-transcript. Invariants restored: the cost of a tab switch does not grow with history length; a bottom reader sees the bottom, never another position, in every painted frame of a switch or a catch-up.',
	status: 'done',
